  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1253 PM MDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nSprawling 598 dam subtropical ridge is centered just south of the\nFour Corners. Despite continued easterly flow aloft, it is\ncarrying in very warm and dry air aloft. 500 mb temp on the 18Z\nRAOB was -3.3 C. The inverted trough at one time associated with\nthe remnants of Bertha is passing a little further north than\npreviously projected, along the Durango/Chihuahua border, but\nstill too far south to have any impact on weather directly. It\nwill help enhance convection over the Sierra Madres, and\nassociated low level moisture will work its way into southwest \nNew Mexico over the weekend.\n\nOtherwise, we are just getting scattered high elevation\nconvection, and will likely see little to no drift into the\nlowlands. Some outflow winds may approach Lordsburg Playa after \nabout 5 or 6 PM. \n\nA slight uptick in convection is expected Saturday, still mainly\nfocused over the higher terrain, but with more drift into the\nlowlands, favoring southwestern New Mexico.\n\nAnother weak inverted south passes south of the area on Sunday,\nbut again mostly influencing weather to our south (but further\nnorth along the Sierra Madres Sunday evening). Convection chances\nlook a little more questionable over our area Sunday and Monday,\nbut moisture from activity in Sonora and Arizona looks to become\nmore firmly in place in SW New Mexico by Tuesday. It still looks\nlike we will be battling dry and warm air intrusions coming in \nfrom Texas through much of next week, with no clear monsoon \n\"bursts\" on the horizon, despite a weakening ridge drifting \nslightly northward.",
